# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  logoscrypt.la — CRYPT_FAST, the fast crypt record (OS_LAYERS_DESIGN.md).
#
#  WHAT. The OS layers above the kernel take their cryptography as ONE
#  record, never by importing the primitives:
#
#      crypt = la s. s(sha256)(hmac)(hkdf)(seal)(open)(hex)
#        sha256(msg)                   32 raw bytes                 (FIPS 180-4)
#        hmac(key)(msg)                32 raw bytes, HMAC-SHA256    (RFC 2104)
#        hkdf(salt)(ikm)(info)(len)    len raw bytes, HKDF-SHA256   (RFC 5869)
#        seal(key)(nonce)(aad)(pt)     ciphertext ++ 16-byte tag    (RFC 8439)
#        open(key)(nonce)(aad)(sealed) la none. la some. ...: none(none) when
#                                      the tag does not verify, some(pt)
#        hex(raw)                      lowercase hex
#
#  crypt_ref.la's CRYPT_REF builds that record from the RFC-verified modules
#  (sha256.la, hmac.la, hkdf.la, chacha20.la, poly1305.la, aead.la). It is
#  the reference, and slow on the VM. CRYPT_FAST has the same contract and is
#  a drop-in for it, with one exception where the reference is wrong (WHERE
#  IT IS NOT CRYPT_REF'S TWIN, below). gate_logoscrypt.sh checks it against
#  the published answers, an independent oracle, and CRYPT_REF itself.
#
#  MEASURED on the VM, timed inside the program by gate_logoscrypt.sh on a
#  loaded 4-core machine: sha256 of 1 KB 8 ms (CRYPT_REF 258 s, 31784x), of
#  10 KB 69 ms (CRYPT_REF 2195 s); seal of 1 KB 8 ms (CRYPT_REF 66 s, 7879x),
#  of 10 KB 126 ms (CRYPT_REF 669 s). Building the record takes about 3 ms.
#  Under heavier load all of these roughly double; the factors hold.
#
#  HOW IT IS USED. Build it ONCE at startup, with the builtins it needs:
#
#      import("logoscrypt.la")
#      ... CRYPT_FAST(str_at)(ord)(str_to_int)(int_to_str)(chr)(concat)(str_len)
#                    (error)(add)(sub)(mul)(lt)(int_eq)(band)(bor)(bxor)
#                    (bshl)(bshr) ...
#
#  and pass the record on as a value, exactly as CRYPT_REF is passed.
#
#  WHY IT IS FAST. On the SECD VM (WM_DESIGN.md, "How fast the VM is"):
#   - naming a builtin or a glyph walks the program, so no hot code names
#     one: every function below is a glyph whose PARAMETERS are the builtins,
#     constants and helpers it uses, applied once when CRYPT_FAST is built;
#   - an application of a builtin costs ~50 ns, but a let (la x. REST)(e)
#     costs ~200 ns, because CLOSE scans REST, and a curried function of n
#     arguments scans its body n-1 times. So the hot functions are small,
#     take few arguments, bind few names, and recompute an addition rather
#     than bind it (measured; the QR below is the fastest of four shapes);
#   - 32-bit words live in 64-bit integers WITHOUT a mask after every add:
#     the low 32 bits of a sum or xor depend only on the low 32 bits of the
#     operands, so junk above bit 31 is harmless until a rotation, and every
#     rotation masks its input first. A rotation is one multiply and one
#     shift: for a clean word w, D = w * (2^32 + 1) holds w in both halves,
#     and rotr(w, n) is the low 32 bits of D >> n;
#   - bytes are read with str_at (O(1)) and written through a 256-byte table
#     T256 (one str_at per byte); strings are joined by balanced halving.
#
#  THE PITFALLS THE ALGORITHMS RECORD, AND WHERE THEY ARE HANDLED
#   - SHA-256 padding: the 0x80 byte and the 64-bit bit length are not
#     copied into the message; the final block's words are read past the end
#     (str_at gives "" there, which reads as 0) with 0x80 OR-ed into its word.
#   - HMAC: a key longer than 64 bytes is hashed first (RFC 2104). The inner
#     digest goes into the outer hash as WORDS, never as hex.
#   - HKDF: an empty salt is 32 zero bytes (RFC 5869 2.2). HMAC pads its key
#     with zeros to 64 bytes, so "" and 32 zero bytes are the same key; the
#     gate checks it. len must be 0..8160 (255 blocks), else a loud error.
#   - ChaCha20-Poly1305: the one-time Poly1305 key is block 0, the keystream
#     starts at counter 1; mac_data is aad, pad16, ct, pad16, len(aad) and
#     len(ct) as 64-bit little-endian; r is clamped (folded into the limb
#     masks); the final reduction subtracts p only when h >= p; + s carries
#     across the four 32-bit words. Poly1305 runs in five 26-bit limbs (the
#     largest intermediate is below 2^58).
#   - open verifies BEFORE it decrypts and never releases unauthenticated
#     bytes: the tag is recomputed over aad and the ciphertext as they stand
#     in `sealed`, compared as four words OR-ed together (no early exit), and
#     only on a match is the keystream generated. A sealed string shorter
#     than 16 bytes is none.
#
#  ENGINES. Pure Lingua Adamica over band/bor/bxor/bshl/bshr: the native SECD
#  VM (its engine) and the C host give the same bytes (the gate checks the
#  host on a small program); the host is far too slow for anything but tiny
#  inputs (about a minute for sha256, HMAC, HKDF and one short seal).
#
#  WHERE IT IS NOT CRYPT_REF'S TWIN. CRYPT_REF's sha256 is wrong for message
#  lengths of 55 (mod 64): sha256.la's PADLEN pads them with 64 zero bytes,
#  where FIPS 180-4 pads none (L + 1 + 0 + 8 = 64), so it hashes one block too
#  many. CRYPT_FAST follows FIPS (hashlib agrees); the two records give
#  different sha256 -- and so HMAC and HKDF -- results at those lengths only.
#  gate_logoscrypt.sh names the reference's digest there exactly.
#
#  HONESTLY OUT OF SCOPE
#   - Constant time. The tag comparison has no early exit, and ChaCha20 and
#     Poly1305 have no data-dependent branches, but the VM allocates and
#     interprets: no timing claim is made about this engine.
#   - Stricter than CRYPT_REF on misuse: seal/open halt with an error when the
#     key is not 32 bytes or the nonce not 12 (CRYPT_REF silently reads a short
#     key as zero-padded), and hkdf halts when len is outside 0..8160
#     (CRYPT_REF halts above 8160 too, but loops forever on a negative len).
#   - Nonce uniqueness is the caller's duty, as in aead.la.
#   - No PBKDF2 or precomputed-key HMAC in the record (the contract has none);
#     internally hkdf reuses one HMAC key schedule for all its blocks.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

export CRYPT_FAST

glyph Z = la f. (la x. f(la v. x(x)(v)))(la x. f(la v. x(x)(v)))

# ── bytes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# sa = str_at(s), partially applied once per string. A byte past the end is
# "" and reads as 0, which is how every reader below zero-fills.
# Big- and little-endian 32-bit words at byte offset o; u = mul(256).
glyph RD_BE = la ord. la si. la p. la C1. la C2. la C3. la u. la sa. la o.
  p(u(p(u(p(u(si(ord(sa(o)))))(si(ord(sa(p(o)(C1)))))))(si(ord(sa(p(o)(C2)))))))(si(ord(sa(p(o)(C3)))))
glyph RD_LE = la ord. la si. la p. la C1. la C2. la C3. la u. la sa. la o.
  p(si(ord(sa(o))))(u(p(si(ord(sa(p(o)(C1)))))(u(p(si(ord(sa(p(o)(C2)))))(u(si(ord(sa(p(o)(C3))))))))))
# a clean word as 4 bytes; T = str_at(T256), n = band(255)
glyph WR_BE = la concat. la s. la C8. la C16. la C24. la n. la T. la w.
  concat(concat(T(s(w)(C24)))(T(n(s(w)(C16)))))(concat(T(n(s(w)(C8))))(T(n(w))))
glyph WR_LE = la concat. la s. la C8. la C16. la C24. la n. la T. la w.
  concat(concat(T(n(w)))(T(n(s(w)(C8)))))(concat(T(n(s(w)(C16))))(T(s(w)(C24))))

# span(f)(lo)(hi): f(lo) ++ ... ++ f(hi-1), joined by halving; "" when lo >= hi
glyph SPAN = la concat. la add. la sub. la lt. la bshr. la C0. la C1. la f.
  Z(la sp. la lo. la hi.
    lt(add(lo)(C1))(hi)
      (la _. (la mid. concat(sp(lo)(mid))(sp(mid)(hi)))(bshr(add(lo)(hi))(C1)))
      (la _. lt(lo)(hi)(la _. f(lo))(la _. "")(C0))
      (C0))

# ── SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Σ0/Σ1: three rotations of the doubled word, xored (junk above bit 31 left).
glyph SHA_SIGMA = la r. la x. la m. la s. la A. la B. la C. la e.
  (la D. x(x(s(D)(A))(s(D)(B)))(s(D)(C)))(r(m(e)))
# σ0/σ1: two rotations and a plain shift of the clean word
glyph SHA_SIG = la r. la x. la m. la s. la A. la B. la C. la w.
  (la v. x(x(s(r(v))(A))(s(r(v))(B)))(s(v)(C)))(m(w))
glyph SHA_CH = la x. la n. la e. la f. la g. x(g)(n(e)(x(f)(g)))
glyph SHA_MAJ = la o. la n. la a. la b. la c. o(n(a)(b))(n(c)(o(a)(b)))

# One round on a state record la k. k(a)(b)(c)(d)(e)(f)(g)(h); w = K[t] + W[t].
# The new record's a and e fields are computed when the next round reads
# them; every state record is read exactly once.
glyph SHA_ROUND = la S0. la S1. la CH. la MJ. la p. la st. la w.
  st(la a. la b. la c. la d. la e. la f. la g. la h.
    (la t. la k. k(p(t)(p(S0(a))(MJ(a)(b)(c))))(a)(b)(c)(p(d)(t))(e)(f)(g))
    (p(p(h)(S1(e)))(p(CH(e)(f)(g))(w))))

# The schedule and the round constants travel as QUADS, la s. s(w0)(w1)(w2)(w3):
# a step of the schedule then binds only the words it reads, instead of
# rebinding a 16-word window per word (measured: a quarter faster per block).
# A quad is read several times, so its words are always computed before it
# is built, never inside it.
#
# W[i] = σ1(W[i-2]) + W[i-7] + σ0(W[i-15]) + W[i-16]
glyph SHA_NW = la s0. la s1. la p. la q. la j. la b. la a. p(p(s1(q))(j))(p(s0(b))(a))
# NEXTQ(q0)(q1)(q2)(q3): from the window W[t..t+15] the next quad W[t+16..t+19]
# (the last two need the first two). h1 picks a quad's first word.
glyph SHA_NEXTQ = la nw. la h1.
  la q0. la q1. la q2. la q3.
  q0(la a. la b. la c. la d. q2(la e. la f. la g. la h. q3(la i. la j. la k. la l.
    (la X. la Y. (la Z. la W. la s. s(X)(Y)(Z)(W))(nw(X)(h)(d)(c))(nw(Y)(i)(q1(h1))(d)))
    (nw(k)(f)(b)(a))(nw(l)(g)(c)(b)))))
# the 16 quads of the schedule as a Scott list: 12 steps, then the last window
glyph SHA_SCHED = la nx. la sub. la ie. la C1. la C0. la nil. la cons.
  Z(la go. la n. la q0. la q1. la q2. la q3.
     cons(q0)(ie(n)(C0)
               (la _. cons(q1)(cons(q2)(cons(q3)(nil))))
               (la _. go(sub(n)(C1))(q1)(q2)(q3)(nx(q0)(q1)(q2)(q3)))
               (C0)))
# the 64 rounds, four per step, over the schedule quads and the K quads
glyph SHA_ROUNDS = la p. la R.
  Z(la go. la st. la ql. la kl.
     ql(la _. st)(la q. la qt. kl(la _. st)(la K. la kt.
       go(q(la a. la b. la c. la d. K(la e. la f. la g. la h.
            R(R(R(R(st)(p(e)(a)))(p(f)(b)))(p(g)(c)))(p(h)(d)))))
         (qt)(kt))))

# H + S, masked: the feed-forward that ends a compression
glyph SHA_ADD8 = la p. la m. la A. la B.
  A(la a. la b. la c. la d. la e. la f. la g. la h. B(la i. la j. la K. la l. la M. la N. la o. la P.
    la k. k(m(p(a)(i)))(m(p(b)(j)))(m(p(c)(K)))(m(p(d)(l)))(m(p(e)(M)))(m(p(f)(N)))(m(p(g)(o)))(m(p(h)(P)))))

# A block, la f. f(q0)(q1)(q2)(q3): words 0..13 from g(byte offset), 14 and
# 15 are x and y. Q builds a quad from four computed words.
glyph SHA_MKBLK = la Q. la C0. la C4. la C8. la C12. la C16. la C20. la C24. la C28.
   la C32. la C36. la C40. la C44. la C48. la C52. la g. la x. la y.
  la f. f(Q(g(C0))(g(C4))(g(C8))(g(C12)))(Q(g(C16))(g(C20))(g(C24))(g(C28)))
         (Q(g(C32))(g(C36))(g(C40))(g(C44)))(Q(g(C48))(g(C52))(x)(y))

# ── ChaCha20 (RFC 8439 2.3) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The quarter round on (a, b, c, d), returning la k. k(a')(b')(c')(d'). Inputs
# may carry junk above bit 31; m masks before every rotation (r doubles,
# s shifts right by 32-n). W X Y V are 25 24 20 16 (rotl 7 8 12 16).
glyph CC_QR = la W. la X. la Y. la V. la r. la s. la x. la p. la m.
  la a. la b. la c. la d.
  (la D. (la B. (la A. (la E. (la C.
      la k. k(A)(s(r(m(x(B)(C))))(W))(C)(E))
    (p(p(c)(D))(E)))
    (s(r(m(x(D)(A))))(X)))
    (p(p(a)(b))(B)))
    (s(r(m(x(b)(p(c)(D)))))(Y)))
  (s(r(m(x(d)(p(a)(b)))))(V))

# One double round. The state is la k. k(col0)(col1)(col2)(col3), column j
# being (x_j, x_4+j, x_8+j, x_12+j). Column round, regroup into diagonals
# (x0 x5 x10 x15) (x1 x6 x11 x12) (x2 x7 x8 x13) (x3 x4 x9 x14), diagonal
# round, regroup back into columns.
glyph CC_DR = la q.
  (la diag. la st. st(la p. la t. la u. la v. diag(p(q))(t(q))(u(q))(v(q))))
  ((la cols. la p. la t. la u. la v.
     p(la a0. la b0. la c0. la d0. t(la a1. la b1. la c1. la d1. u(la a2. la b2. la c2. la d2. v(la a3. la b3. la c3. la d3.
       cols(q(a0)(b1)(c2)(d3))(q(a1)(b2)(c3)(d0))(q(a2)(b3)(c0)(d1))(q(a3)(b0)(c1)(d2)))))))
   (la p. la t. la u. la v.
     p(la a0. la b1. la c2. la d3. t(la a1. la b2. la c3. la d0. u(la a2. la b3. la c0. la d1. v(la a3. la b0. la c1. la d2.
       la k. k(la k. k(a0)(b0)(c0)(d0))(la k. k(a1)(b1)(c1)(d1))(la k. k(a2)(b2)(c2)(d2))(la k. k(a3)(b3)(c3)(d3))))))))

# Twenty rounds, then hand the 16 keystream words (final + initial, masked)
# to f in order x0..x15. The key words, nonce words and counter are the
# initial state.
glyph CC_BLOCK = la p. la m. la X0. la X1. la X2. la X3. la dr.
  la k0. la k1. la k2. la k3. la k4. la k5. la k6. la k7. la n0. la n1. la n2. la ctr. la f.
  dr(dr(dr(dr(dr(dr(dr(dr(dr(dr(
     la k. k(la k. k(X0)(k0)(k4)(ctr))(la k. k(X1)(k1)(k5)(n0))(la k. k(X2)(k2)(k6)(n1))(la k. k(X3)(k3)(k7)(n2))))))))))))
  (la P. la T. la U. la V.
    P(la a0. la b0. la c0. la d0. T(la a1. la b1. la c1. la d1. U(la a2. la b2. la c2. la d2. V(la a3. la b3. la c3. la d3.
      f(m(p(a0)(X0)))(m(p(a1)(X1)))(m(p(a2)(X2)))(m(p(a3)(X3)))
       (m(p(b0)(k0)))(m(p(b1)(k1)))(m(p(b2)(k2)))(m(p(b3)(k3)))
       (m(p(c0)(k4)))(m(p(c1)(k5)))(m(p(c2)(k6)))(m(p(c3)(k7)))
       (m(p(d0)(ctr)))(m(p(d1)(n0)))(m(p(d2)(n1)))(m(p(d3)(n2))))))))

# 64 bytes of keystream XOR the word reader g (g(i) = the LE word at chunk
# byte offset i), as a string: the 16 words, joined by halving.
glyph CC_XOR64 = la concat. la x. la wr. la C0. la C4. la C8. la C12. la C16. la C20. la C24. la C28.
   la C32. la C36. la C40. la C44. la C48. la C52. la C56. la C60. la g.
  la w0. la w1. la w2. la w3. la w4. la w5. la w6. la w7. la w8. la w9. la wa. la wb. la wc. la wd. la we. la wf.
  concat(concat(concat(concat(wr(x(w0)(g(C0))))(wr(x(w1)(g(C4)))))(concat(wr(x(w2)(g(C8))))(wr(x(w3)(g(C12))))))
                (concat(concat(wr(x(w4)(g(C16))))(wr(x(w5)(g(C20)))))(concat(wr(x(w6)(g(C24))))(wr(x(w7)(g(C28)))))))
        (concat(concat(concat(wr(x(w8)(g(C32))))(wr(x(w9)(g(C36)))))(concat(wr(x(wa)(g(C40))))(wr(x(wb)(g(C44))))))
                (concat(concat(wr(x(wc)(g(C48))))(wr(x(wd)(g(C52)))))(concat(wr(x(we)(g(C56))))(wr(x(wf)(g(C60)))))))


# ── Poly1305 (RFC 8439 2.5) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Absorb one 16-byte block into h = la k. k(h0)(h1)(h2)(h3)(h4), five 26-bit
# limbs: h := (h + m) * r mod 2^130 - 5. The block is four clean LE words
# t0..t3 and always carries the high bit 2^128 (HB = 2^24 in limb 4): in the
# AEAD every block is a whole one, pad16 makes it so. r0..r4 are the clamped
# key limbs, q1..q4 = 5*r1..5*r4 (2^130 = 5 mod p folds the wrap into the
# multiply). n = band(2^26 - 1), i = mul(5). The products stay below 2^58.
# After the carry, limbs 0, 2, 3, 4 are below 2^26 and limb 1 just above,
# as in poly1305.la, which P_FINAL relies on.
glyph P_ABS = la C26. la C6. la C20. la C12. la C14. la C18. la C8. la HB. la i. la n. la o. la sl. la sr. la p. la mu.
   la r0. la r1. la r2. la r3. la r4. la q1. la q2. la q3. la q4.
   la h. la t0. la t1. la t2. la t3.
  h(la h0. la h1. la h2. la h3. la h4.
    (la a0. la a1. la a2. la a3. la a4.
       (la d0. la d1. la d2. la d3. la d4.
          (la x1. (la x2. (la x3. (la x4. (la f0.
               la k. k(n(f0))(p(n(x1))(sr(f0)(C26)))(n(x2))(n(x3))(n(x4)))
             (p(n(d0))(i(sr(x4)(C26)))))
             (p(d4)(sr(x3)(C26))))
             (p(d3)(sr(x2)(C26))))
             (p(d2)(sr(x1)(C26))))
           (p(d1)(sr(d0)(C26))))
        (p(p(p(p(mu(a0)(r0))(mu(a1)(q4)))(mu(a2)(q3)))(mu(a3)(q2)))(mu(a4)(q1)))
        (p(p(p(p(mu(a0)(r1))(mu(a1)(r0)))(mu(a2)(q4)))(mu(a3)(q3)))(mu(a4)(q2)))
        (p(p(p(p(mu(a0)(r2))(mu(a1)(r1)))(mu(a2)(r0)))(mu(a3)(q4)))(mu(a4)(q3)))
        (p(p(p(p(mu(a0)(r3))(mu(a1)(r2)))(mu(a2)(r1)))(mu(a3)(r0)))(mu(a4)(q4)))
        (p(p(p(p(mu(a0)(r4))(mu(a1)(r3)))(mu(a2)(r2)))(mu(a3)(r1)))(mu(a4)(r0))))
     (p(h0)(n(t0)))
     (p(h1)(n(o(sr(t0)(C26))(sl(t1)(C6)))))
     (p(h2)(n(o(sr(t1)(C20))(sl(t2)(C12)))))
     (p(h3)(n(o(sr(t2)(C14))(sl(t3)(C18)))))
     (p(h4)(o(sr(t3)(C8))(HB))))

# The tag: carry fully, keep h - p when it does not go negative (h was in
# [p, 2^130)), pack into four 32-bit words, add s = s0..s3 mod 2^128 with
# the carry rippling up, and hand the four tag words to f. Transcribed step
# for step from poly1305.la's FINAL.
glyph P_FINAL = la C0. la C5. la C6. la C8. la C12. la C14. la C18. la C20. la C32. la C2_26. la C26.
   la lt. la sub. la i. la o. la sl. la m. la n. la sr. la p.
   la s0. la s1. la s2. la s3. la h. la f.
  h(la a0. la a1. la a2. la a3. la a4.
    (la b2. (la b3. (la b4. (la b0.
      (la f0. la f1. la f2. la f3. la f4.
        (la g0. (la g1. (la g2. (la g3. (la g4.
          (la e.
            (la p0. la p1. la p2. la p3. la p4.
              (la w0. la w1. la w2. la w3.
                (la u0. (la u1. (la u2.
                    f(m(u0))(m(u1))(m(u2))(m(p(p(w3)(s3))(sr(u2)(C32)))))
                  (p(p(w2)(s2))(sr(u1)(C32))))
                  (p(p(w1)(s1))(sr(u0)(C32))))
                  (p(w0)(s0)))
              (m(o(p0)(sl(p1)(C26))))
              (m(o(sr(p1)(C6))(sl(p2)(C20))))
              (m(o(sr(p2)(C12))(sl(p3)(C14))))
              (m(o(sr(p3)(C18))(sl(p4)(C8)))))
            (e(f0)(n(g0)))(e(f1)(n(g1)))(e(f2)(n(g2)))(e(f3)(n(g3)))(e(f4)(g4)))
          (la x. la y. lt(g4)(C0)(la _. x)(la _. y)(C0)))
        (sub(p(f4)(sr(g3)(C26)))(C2_26)))
        (p(f3)(sr(g2)(C26))))
        (p(f2)(sr(g1)(C26))))
        (p(f1)(sr(g0)(C26))))
        (p(f0)(C5)))
      (n(b0))(p(n(a1))(sr(b0)(C26)))(n(b2))(n(b3))(n(b4)))
    (p(a0)(i(sr(b4)(C26)))))
    (p(a4)(sr(b3)(C26))))
    (p(a3)(sr(b2)(C26))))
    (p(a2)(sr(a1)(C26))))

# Poly1305 over the first L bytes of a string (sa = str_at of it), zero-padded
# to 16: whole blocks read directly; the last partial block's words are masked
# to the bytes that belong to the segment, because in `open` the bytes after
# the ciphertext are the tag, not zeros.
